# Render, integrate, execute

**Render, integrate, execute** names three operations that the discussion
separates when the output of a computation is itself a document: rendering a
result as Markdown, integrating that Markdown into the vault, and executing
any cells it contains. Each is a distinct step that needs its own decision.
The separation is a design proposed in the discussion, not an established
term.

## Description

### The observation that prompted it

The starting remark was short: output can be Markdown as well. If a cell
evaluates to Markdown or HMD, its result may contain headings, links, whole
cards, and new fenced cells. Data has become code, and running that code
yields data again.

````markdown
# Observation 4
The previous experiment revealed a discrepancy.

## Proposed next step
```text
(a generated cell, pending validation)
```
````

A system in which this happens without control executes whatever it writes.
The three operations are how control is kept.

### The three operations

1. **Render.** Turn a computational result into Markdown. Nothing changes in
   the vault. The output is inert text.
2. **Integrate.** Accept the Markdown as knowledge, or as a proposed revision
   of a source. The vault changes, under version control.
3. **Execute.** Run cells from the new content, and only those intentionally
   selected.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[HMD source] --> B[HQL evaluation]
 B --> C[Generated HMD]
 C --> D{Approved for integration?}
 D -->|yes| E[Versioned HMD graph]
 D -->|no| F[Keep as inert output]
 E --> G{Explicit next execution?}
 G -->|yes| B
```

There are two gates. Generated content does not enter the vault unless
approved, and content that has entered does not run unless explicitly chosen.
Output that fails the first gate is kept, as output.

### The formal model

$$H_{n+1}=\operatorname{Integrate}(H_n,\operatorname{Eval}(H_n)).$$

The next state of the vault is the current state together with whatever was
accepted from its own evaluation. Rendering is hidden inside
$\operatorname{Eval}$, and execution is the fact that $H_{n+1}$ may be
evaluated in turn. This is the [[hmd-feedback-loop]] stated as a recurrence.

### What it amounts to

The discussion's verdict is measured. This is a "reflective programming
substrate", a base on which reflection can be built. It qualifies as a
[[strange-loop]] only when the relevant cross-level self-reference is
demonstrated. A vault that generates and re-runs its own cells may still be
plain feedback.

The essay on AI-oriented languages meets the same design from the other side.
Its chain from intention to specification to program to execution to
observation to revised specification has the same joints, and it names the
engineering questions that the gates answer: validation, permissions,
provenance, versioning, and the limits of self-modification. It also supplies
the reason the first gate cannot be automated away. A checker can prove that
a program follows a specification. It cannot prove that the specification is
what was wanted.

### In this vault

Rendering a card prints its cells' results and changes nothing, which is the
first operation alone. Writing results back into the card is a deliberate
option of the renderer, and running a query marked as a future design is
forbidden outright. The convention that a future query is never run is the
third gate applied by hand.

## Relations

- It is the safety discipline of the [[hmd-feedback-loop]] {relation: part-of}.
- Its two gates are held by a [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: uses} or an agent under review.
- Its integrate step is a [[revision]] {relation: is-a} of the vault.
- Its gates are acts of [[evaluation]] {relation: uses}: a claim, a criterion, an assessment.
- It generalizes the boundaries of the [[observer-card-experiment]] {relation: generalizes}.
- It is not by itself a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a substrate for reflection, not reflection.
- Without the gates it would be a self-executing [[feedback-loop]] {relation: contrasts}.
- It keeps apart [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses}: text that is about the vault, and text that is the vault.
- It bears on the question [[ai-support-in-hql]] {relation: answers}: any agent output in a cell passes these gates.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[hmd-feedback-loop]].** Card, cell, output, interpretation, revision.
  This card describes what must happen between its output and revision
  steps.
- **[[human-in-the-loop]].** A person at the step where a result is
  interpreted. Here the person has two precise decisions: integrate, and
  execute.
- **[[observer-card-experiment]].** One card inspecting a pinned revision of
  itself. Its five execution boundaries are these gates in a single card.
- **[[revision]].** The operation from a model and evidence to an updated
  model. Integration is revision with a version history.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[render-integrate-execute]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[render-integrate-execute]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

